From 3c8cc605a3f72b4a3d37b2358ea3eb6d981ee40f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 18:35:18 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Detile MediaTek MM21 on the CPU into dma-buf heap NV12

MediaTek stateless decoders such as mt6797-vdec output only MM21: two
non-contiguous planes of 16x32 luma tiles and 16x16 interleaved CbCr
tiles. No GPU, display controller or client can use it, and on MT6797
there is no mem2mem device to convert it, so the existing converter
chain refused such streams.

Add MM21 to the format table as a decoder-only format and give the
converter chain a software mode for it:

- The surface backing is linear NV12 allocated from
  /dev/dma_heap/system, so it exports as a dma-buf like the RGA
  backing. Its pitch is aligned to 64 bytes and it covers the
  decoder's padded size; the chroma plane follows the luma rows.
- The CPU detiles each frame (mm21.c), streaming each tile of the
  uncached decoder buffer sequentially, bracketed with
  DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC on the decoder planes and on the backing. The
  write sync also waits for a GPU still reading the previous frame.
- Conversion happens on the first read through v4l2r_surface_ready(),
  and vaEndPicture() waits for the decode and the conversion of its
  target. Chromium exports each surface once and never calls
  vaSyncSurface(), so the backing must be current when vaEndPicture()
  returns. A frame overwritten before it was read is not converted.
- When no decoder can produce NV12, bare surfaces (exported before
  their first decode) get heap backing in the same layout, so a
  dma-buf a client exported early stays the one that is updated.

References stay in the decoder's MM21 buffers. vaDeriveImage and
vaGetImage read the NV12 backing.
