+MediaTek MM21 and the software converter
+----------------------------------------
+
+Some MediaTek decoders (e.g. mt6797-vdec) output only MM21: two planes
+of 16x32 luma and 16x16 interleaved CbCr tiles. Nothing on such SoCs can
+import it, and there is no converter device, so the driver detiles MM21
+on the CPU:
+
+- Each surface gets a linear NV12 backing from /dev/dma_heap/system
+  (CONFIG_DMABUF_HEAPS_SYSTEM), exported like any other NV12 surface.
+  Bare surfaces get the same layout when no decoder can produce NV12,
+  so a dma-buf exported before the first decode stays valid.
+- vaEndPicture() waits for the decode and detiles the frame, bracketed
+  with DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC on both buffers. Clients such as Chromium
+  export each surface once and never call vaSyncSurface(), so the
+  backing has to be current when vaEndPicture() returns.
+- Reference frames stay in the decoder's MM21 buffers.
+- vaDeriveImage()/vaGetImage() read the NV12 backing.
+
+The cost is one CPU copy per frame (about 3 MiB at 1080p), read from the
+decoder's uncached buffer.
+
+
 Video processing: rotation and mirroring
